# The Guide tab: what each tab needs, what it gives, and what to check. Plain text, kept next to the code it describes.
# Sections follow the order of the top bar; a thin line separates them; the text column is kept narrow enough to read.

guide_section = function(title, needs, gives, check) tagList(
  tags$hr(style = "margin: 1.4rem 0 1.1rem; border-top: 1px solid #D5D9DE; opacity: 1;"),
  h5(title),
  tags$dl(class = "row mb-1",
    tags$dt(class = "col-sm-2", "You load"), tags$dd(class = "col-sm-10", needs),
    tags$dt(class = "col-sm-2", "You get"), tags$dd(class = "col-sm-10", gives),
    tags$dt(class = "col-sm-2", "Check"), tags$dd(class = "col-sm-10", check)))

guide_ui = function() nav_panel("Guide",
  card(div(style = "max-width: 900px; margin: 0 auto; width: 100%;",
    h4("How to use ElectroFast"),
    p("Load your files on a tab, set the options in its sidebar, and read the result next to it. Every tab with data goes into the",
      "report (HTML, PDF or PowerPoint) on the Report tab. Nothing you load is kept on the server after you close the page."),
    p("Closing or reloading the page after loading a file asks for confirmation first. Your files and settings are also kept in",
      "this browser on this computer. If the page is closed by mistake, the next visit offers to restore or discard them."),
    h5("Example data"),
    p("Simulated files with known answers, for trying the app or checking that it works. They are cyclic voltammograms from slow to fast",
      "kinetics, single-entity spikes and steps, a Cottrell transient, an impedance spectrum, two precision studies and a QC history. They contain no measured data.",
      "The README in the zip says which tab to use, which settings to enter and what the app should show for each file.",
      tags$a(href = "examples/ElectroFast_example_data_simulated.zip", download = "ElectroFast_example_data_simulated.zip",
             "Download the example data (zip, 11 MB)")),
    h5("Files"),
    p("CH Instruments text exports, with or without the header (the data-only layouts of every technique are recognised). PalmSens",
      "PSTrace sessions (.pssession). BioLogic EC-Lab text (.mpt) files (for a binary .mpr file, export the run as .mpt in EC-Lab).",
      "Gamry Framework files (.DTA). Pine",
      "AfterMath text exports. Lawson Labs EMF16 recordings for potentiometry. Any other text or CSV file with a header row, where the",
      "columns are found by their names and units. Units are read from each file, never assumed."),
    p("The Instrument choice at the top right is set to Auto-detect, which recognises the instrument from each file. Choosing an",
      "instrument makes the technique tabs use that reader only. A file from another instrument then gives a message saying what it",
      "looks like. EMF16 recordings for potentiometry are recognised on the ISE tabs."),
    p("Next to it, \"Name on figures\" adds a small grey \"ElectroFast\" at the lower right of every plot. Untick it for figures",
      "with no added text, for example for a paper. The choice applies to the plots on screen, the downloaded figures and the reports."),
    p("\"Label plots\" (off by default) adds labels taken from the data. On a voltammogram: an arrow beside the last forward and",
      "return sweeps of the first file, showing the direction of the scan, and the peak current with its potential. On",
      "Multi-Potential Steps traces: the potential of each step from the file settings, the cycle numbers and the reading windows",
      "of the cycles used. Untick it for plain figures."),
    h5("Electrode area"),
    p("Every tab that needs an area has an Electrode panel. Pick a standard disk, enter any disk or microdisk diameter, choose another",
      "shape (plate, strip, wire, band, hemisphere or sphere) or type the area in. The geometric area is shown under the panel."),
    h4("Techniques", style = "margin-top: 2rem;"),
    guide_section("Cyclic voltammetry: Peaks and kinetics",
      "CVs of a redox probe (or any couple) at several scan rates. For an area, a solution with only one form of the couple (for example 1 mM ferricyanide alone).",
      HTML("Peak potentials and currents and the peak separation. Anodic and cathodic peak currents against v and v<sup>1/2</sup> (slope, intercept and R²), and the log-log slope of each.",
           "The transfer coefficient α, from the peak width for irreversible waves and from the K table for quasi-reversible ones. k<sup>0</sup> by the Nicholson-Lavagnini, Klingler-Kochi and K-table methods.",
           "The electroactive area or D by Randles–Ševčík in its reversible, quasi-reversible (K per scan rate) or irreversible form. Surface coverage and the Laviron plot for adsorbed species."),
      "Choose the form from the peak separation in the table: up to 63/n mV reversible, 63 to 200 mV quasi-reversible, above 200 mV irreversible. Tick the mixture box when both forms are in solution: the tab then gives no area and points to the potential-step tabs."),
    guide_section("Cyclic voltammetry: ECSA",
      "Choose the electrode material first. Then load a CV in a blank electrolyte through the surface process. This is the gold oxide for gold, hydrogen, oxide or CO stripping for Pt, Rh and Ir, PdO reduction for Pd, and underpotential deposition of Cu, Ag or Pb.",
      HTML("Gold: the oxide reduction peak found automatically on the last cathodic sweep, the area (charge / 390 µC/cm²) and the roughness factor.",
           "Pt, Pd, Rh, Ir: the charge between two potentials you set, above a straight baseline, over the charge density of the chosen reference.",
           "Glassy carbon and other carbons, ITO and other surfaces have no surface reaction with a known charge. The tab points to a redox probe",
           "(<i>Peaks and kinetics</i> or <i>Chronoamperometry</i>) or to <i>Double-layer capacitance</i>."),
      "The shaded or windowed peak covers the whole surface peak and nothing else, and the charge density matches the metal and the process (the Equations tab gives the sources)."),
    guide_section("Cyclic voltammetry: Double-layer capacitance",
      "CVs at several scan rates over a range with no faradaic peaks.",
      HTML("C<sub>dl</sub> from the slope of the half difference of anodic and cathodic currents against scan rate, and a rough area from C<sub>dl</sub> / C<sub>s</sub>, the specific capacitance."),
      HTML("The currents are flat over the chosen potential. C<sub>s</sub> (20 to 40 µF/cm² for most surfaces) is an assumption, so treat the area as approximate.")),
    guide_section("Linear sweep voltammetry: Tafel",
      "A slow LSV or CV.",
      HTML("The Tafel slope in a chosen window, αn and the exchange current, with a scan of every window so the choice can be seen."),
      "The slope depends strongly on the window. A window of a few mV is not enough to define it."),
    guide_section("Linear sweep voltammetry: Rotating disk and RRDE",
      "LSVs at several rotation rates (rpm in the file names). For RRDE, disk and ring currents.",
      "Levich and Koutecky-Levich analysis, collection efficiency, faradaic efficiency, electron number and peroxide yield.",
      "The limiting currents form a plateau. The collection efficiency is measured with a known couple before the unknown runs."),
    guide_section("Pulse voltammetry (DPV, SWV)",
      "One pulse voltammogram per concentration. Concentrations come from the file names or are typed in the Concentrations table.",
      "The current at a set potential or the peak in a window, the calibration line, R², range and detection limit.",
      "Every concentration is plotted, including the ones left out of the fit. For square wave, the difference current is used."),
    guide_section("Chronoamperometry",
      "i-t, chronoamperometry or Multi-Potential Steps exports, or Axon pCLAMP (.abf) recordings from a patch-clamp amplifier.",
      "One tab per analysis as thus: Multi-Potential Steps and step currents with calibration, charge passed, Cottrell (D or area), microelectrode steady state, single-entity spikes and single-entity steps. Spikes are reported by count, frequency, amplitude, duration and charge. Steps are lasting current changes, as when particles land and stay active, and are reported by count, frequency and height.",
      "For Cottrell, the fitted time window starts after the charging current and before convection. For spikes and steps, set the thresholds before looking at the result. Long recordings are drawn from the highest and lowest point of each short interval, so every spike and step stays visible. Four more views help to judge the data. Zoom shows every point in a narrow window (drag to zoom). Event explorer plots events over time, size over time, size against duration and the size distribution. File check reports the sampling, noise, net change, gaps and clipping. Compare files subtracts a reference, such as a blank, from each file."),
    guide_section("Chronocoulometry",
      "A potential step with the analyte, and the same step in electrolyte alone.",
      "The Anson plot: area or D from the slope, and the adsorbed amount from the intercept minus the double-layer charge of the blank step.",
      "The step starts at the rest potential and ends where the reaction is fully driven. Valid in a solution with both forms of a couple."),
    guide_section("Chronopotentiometry",
      "CP runs at one or more currents.",
      "Transition times, the Sand line and concentrations.",
      "Each run continues past the transition, otherwise there is no transition time to read."),
    guide_section("Open-circuit potential",
      "OCP recordings from a potentiostat (three-electrode cell: working, reference and counter electrodes).",
      "The final, mean and extreme potentials and the drift over the last part of the run.",
      "The drift is small enough for the purpose before the value is used."),
    guide_section("Potentiometry: ISE calibration, Selectivity and Precision",
      "EMF recordings of a two-electrode cell, the ion-selective electrode against a reference electrode (for example on an EMF16 potentiometer). One file per standard, coded by letter, over one or more days.",
      "The calibration slope (mean ± SD over days), range, R² and % of Nernstian, and the background-corrected fit. The reading-time analysis, selectivity coefficients by the fixed interference and separate solution methods, and repeatability and intermediate precision from a mixed model. A Possible causes tab checks each day for six signs and lists what to check. The signs are a low or high slope, a flattened low end, a rising background, check-standard drift, scatter and unsettled readings.",
      "The same reading time is used for every standard. Precision needs at least 5 days with 2 or more readings per day."),
    guide_section("Impedance (EIS)",
      "One spectrum (frequency, Z′, Z″).",
      HTML("A Kramers-Kronig check, fits of standard and custom circuits ranked by AICc, parameters with standard errors, k<sup>0</sup> from R<sub>ct</sub>, effective capacitance of a CPE, D from the Warburg coefficient, and an interpretation."),
      "The Kramers-Kronig residuals are small before any fit is trusted. A parameter with a large standard error, or at its bound, is not determined by the data."),
    h4("Sensor performance", style = "margin-top: 2rem;"),
    p("These tabs take results of any technique (a peak current, a charge, a potential) as a small CSV table. Each tab has an",
      "empty template with the column names. Units in brackets after a column name are allowed."),
    guide_section("Sensitivity",
      "Concentration and signal of each standard, with blanks as concentration 0.",
      "The sensitivity (slope ± SE), linear range, R², LOD and LOQ, and the slope per area if an area is given.",
      "The noise s comes from two or more blanks, otherwise from the residual SD of the line. Choose the linear range from the plot."),
    guide_section("Selectivity",
      "For each interferent, the signal of the target alone (at the middle of its expected range) and with the interferent added at its expected concentration.",
      "The change of the target signal in percent, and whether it is within the tolerance you set.",
      "Report the interferent concentration with the result: an interferent within the tolerance at one concentration may not be at a higher one."),
    guide_section("Specificity",
      "The signal of the target and of each other species alone, at the same concentration.",
      "The cross-reactivity of each species as a percentage of the target, and the selectivity ratio (target signal / species signal), as in Thévenot et al. 1999.",
      "IUPAC recommends the term selectivity, which can be graded, and discourages specificity for the same concept. Few methods, if any, are specific (Vessman et al. 2001)."),
    guide_section("Stability",
      "The signal of one standard at intervals (days, hours or cycles).",
      "The signal retained as a percentage of the first, the straight-line change per unit time and when it first falls below the limit.",
      "Measure the same standard in the same way each time, so the change reflects the sensor and not the measurement."),
    guide_section("Speed",
      "One recording (EMF16, or a potentiostat export) with an addition, and the time of the addition.",
      "The baseline, final level, change, and the response times t90 and t95.",
      "The final level is the median over the end of the recording: the signal must have settled by then, or t90 and t95 are too short."),
    guide_section("Precision",
      "Repeated results with any of the columns day, electrode and laboratory.",
      "Repeatability, between-day and between-electrode SD, intermediate precision, and reproducibility when two or more laboratories are present (ICH Q2(R2) terms), each with its RSD.",
      "Five or more levels of each grouping are usually needed for a reliable SD. Electrode-to-electrode variation is part of the intermediate precision, not the reproducibility."),
    h4("Other tabs", style = "margin-top: 2rem;"),
    guide_section("QC charts",
      "A QC history CSV from an earlier visit, the results of this session (one click), or values typed in.",
      "Control charts with limits from a reference period and the Westgard rules (1-3s, 2-2s, R-4s, 4-1s, 10x), with things to check when a rule breaks.",
      "Set the reference period to sessions known to be good. Download the history at the end: it is not kept on the server."),
    guide_section("Cell diagram",
      "The cell type (three electrodes, two compartments, rotating disk, or two electrodes on a patch-clamp amplifier), the electrodes, the electrolyte or solutions, an optional analyte and the instrument, typed in. A photo of the cell is optional and only shown alongside.",
      "A schematic (PNG, PDF or SVG) with each part labelled beside the drawing, drawn only from what was entered, and optionally a section in the report.",
      "A blank field shows only the role (for example \"Working electrode\"). Compare the drawing with the cell before using it."),
    guide_section("Report",
      "Nothing new: it collects every tab that has data.",
      "An HTML, PDF or PowerPoint report, and a reproducibility bundle (every setting, file checksums, package versions, every results table and the code).",
      "The version and file checksums at the top of the report identify exactly what was analysed and with which version."),
    tags$hr(style = "margin: 1.4rem 0 1.1rem; border-top: 1px solid #D5D9DE; opacity: 1;"),
    p("Every equation and its source is on the Equations tab under Techniques.")
  )))
